  SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

  Restricted AVF permission allowlist for privileged host apps.

  ============================================================================
  HOW AVF PERMISSIONS ACTUALLY RESOLVE — read before editing
  ============================================================================
  virtualizationservice does not trust SELinux alone; it asks
  PermissionManagerService whether the calling UID holds the relevant
  restricted permission. The three permissions that matter:

    android.permission.MANAGE_VIRTUAL_MACHINE
        signature|privileged. Create / start / stop / query any VM built from an
        app config. Introduced at API level 34. On Android 14 it could be
        granted only to privileged apps; from Android 15 it is available to all
        preinstalled apps.

    android.permission.USE_CUSTOM_VIRTUAL_MACHINE
        signature|privileged. Required for VirtualMachineRawConfig, for a custom
        guest kernel image, for device assignment, and for the `configPath`
        form of the Microdroid payload config — which is the form X-ROM uses,
        because a JSON config inside the payload APK is measured into the VM
        identity while an ad-hoc parameter struct is not.

    android.permission.MANAGE_VIRTUALIZATION_STATE
        signature|privileged. Set the global virtualization state (for example,
        suspend every VM under thermal or memory pressure).

  xrom_avfd does NOT appear in this file. It runs as uid system (AID 1000), and
  PermissionManagerService grants every permission to uid 0 and uid 1000, so an
  allowlist entry for it would be dead text. Its isolation comes from
  sepolicy/system_ext_private/xrom_avfd.te instead — a domain that cannot reach
  /dev/kvm, cannot ptrace, and can only talk to virtualizationservice.

  ============================================================================
  STATUS: forward-looking
  ============================================================================
  Session 1 ships no privileged host app. This file is installed now so that
  the grant path is already in place and enforced (ro.control_privapp_permissions
  = enforce) when the X-Vault host app lands. Rename the package below to match
  the real applicationId; an entry for a package that is not installed is
  harmless, but a privileged app requesting these permissions with no matching
  entry is a boot-time denial.
-->
<permissions>
    <privapp-permissions package="com.xrom.xvault.host">
        <permission name="android.permission.MANAGE_VIRTUAL_MACHINE" />
        <permission name="android.permission.USE_CUSTOM_VIRTUAL_MACHINE" />
        <permission name="android.permission.MANAGE_VIRTUALIZATION_STATE" />
    </privapp-permissions>
</permissions>
